KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp20 --unwind 4
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] .* coroutine: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] .* WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
--
g++ 13 (-fsanitize=address,undefined) and clang++ 18 accept this program,
and it fails only its WRONG assertion.  CBMC fails the first assertion:
coroutines are parsed but not implemented.  The parser keeps only the
operand of `co_await e' and `co_yield e' and turns `co_return [e];' into a
skip, so count() runs as an ordinary function that evaluates 1 and 2 and
returns an unconstrained gen; resume, destroy and promise call
__builtin_coro_resume, _destroy and _promise, which have no body.  None
of the promise's members (get_return_object, initial_suspend,
yield_value, final_suspend) is called.

A fix implements N5008 [dcl.fct.def.coroutine]/5-11 (the replacement
body with the promise object, the initial and final await expressions,
the coroutine state allocated with operator new and freed on destroy),
[expr.await]/3-5 (await_ready, await_suspend, await_resume around a
suspend point), [expr.yield]/1 (co_yield e as co_await
promise.yield_value(e)), [stmt.return.coroutine] (return_value or
return_void, then the final suspend point), and the coroutine_handle
builtins ([coroutine.handle.resumption]).  Suspension needs the coroutine
lowered to a state machine: a frame holding the promise, the parameters,
the locals live across a suspend point and a resume index, and a resume
function that dispatches on the index.  It is KNOWNBUG, not FUTURE, as
the program is accepted and its coroutine semantics silently dropped.

Since coroutines are reported as unsupported, the call of the coroutine
also fails the property "coroutines are not supported" and ends the path.
